Smallest change: two parts, three functions. No new action, route, state file, event type, env switch, workflow or page change. Do not touch `healReason`, `bringBack`, `maybeHeal`, `darkPeriods`, `fleet_events`, `healApp`, `worker.py` or the stand-in worker. Find code by function name (another change is in flight in the same parts).

STEP 1. /Users/akuppala/Documents/26aiRAG/spark-console/service/src/19-watcher.mjs: replace the whole `noteSparkStates` function (and the one comment line above it) with:

// Sparks coming and going (from the status check every 30 seconds).
// A boot time that moved less than this is the same boot (a reading is seconds old by the time it gets here).
const BOOT_SLACK_MS = 120000;
function noteSparkStates(nodes) {
  for (const n of nodes) {
    const s = (knowledge.sparks[n.id] ||= {}), online = n.state === 'online', now = new Date().toISOString(), seenBefore = s.lastSeenAt || null;
    if (online) {
      s.lastSeenAt = now;
      s.gpu = { at: now, busy: reading(n.gpuUtilization, 1, 100), watts: reading(n.gpuPowerWatts, 1, 2000), tempC: reading(n.temperature, 1, 150), upSeconds: reading(n.uptimeSeconds, 1, 1e9) };
    }
    // Going dark keeps the last readings (under three minutes old); coming back says how long it has been up.
    const flipped = s.online !== undefined && s.online !== online;
    if (flipped) fleetEvent(online ? { type: 'spark-online', spark: n.id, upSeconds: s.gpu?.upSeconds ?? null } : { type: 'spark-offline', spark: n.id, last: lastReading(s) });
    s.online = online;
    // A restart Spark Console didn't watch (it was down itself, as in a power cut that took every Spark; or the Spark
    // was back before it counted as dark): the Spark's uptime says when it booted. A boot later than the one on record,
    // with no came-back on record since that boot, gets the two events that were missed (inferred: true; the offline one
    // stamped with when the Spark was last seen, never later than its boot), so what should be running there comes back
    // as after any power-on (healReason, 20-heal.mjs). Once per boot: the boot time on record only moves later, and a
    // new one is saved at once. (Date.parse(now), the clock the events are stamped with; not Date.now().)
    const up = online ? s.gpu.upSeconds : null, boot = up === null ? null : Date.parse(now) - up * 1000, had = Date.parse(s.bootAt) || 0;
    let newBoot = false;
    if (boot !== null && boot > had) {
      newBoot = !had || boot - had > BOOT_SLACK_MS;
      s.bootAt = new Date(boot).toISOString();
      if (had && newBoot && !((cameBack(n.id)?.on || 0) >= boot)) {
        fleetEvent({ type: 'spark-offline', spark: n.id, at: new Date(Math.min(Date.parse(seenBefore) || boot, boot)).toISOString(), last: null, inferred: true });
        fleetEvent({ type: 'spark-online', spark: n.id, upSeconds: up, inferred: true });
      }
    }
    // Written at once, so a console restart while a Spark is dark doesn't announce it going dark again, and a restart
    // found from the boot time isn't found again.
    if (flipped || newBoot) saveKnowledge();
  }
}

Keep exactly, do not "improve":
a) `boot` uses `Date.parse(now)`, never `Date.now()` (the tests move `Date.now()` by up to 13 hours; `new Date()` is not moved, and event times use it).
b) `s.bootAt` moves later on every reading (memory only); it is saved only when `newBoot`. This is what keeps the stand-in fleet quiet (its uptime is a constant).
c) The pair is written only when a boot was already on record (`had`), the new one is more than 2 minutes later, and `cameBack` has no came-back at or after it. A Spark seen for the first time (also the first status check after this install) only gets its boot recorded.
d) Offline first, then online, back to back. `fleetEvent` spreads its argument after its own `at`, so passing `at` back-dates the offline event; that is intended.
e) `cameBack` and `HEAL_OFFLINE_MS` live in 20-heal.mjs and are used here only inside the function at run time (as `darkPeriods` already does), so R28 holds.

