      const own = (spark, model, manifest) => writeFileSync(join(root, spark, 'models', model, 'spark.json'), JSON.stringify(manifest));
      for (const s of ['spark2', 'spark3']) own(s, 'gpt-oss-20b', { title: 'gpt-oss 20B', engine: 'vllm', image: IMAGE, args: [], memoryGiB: 24 });
      for (const s of ['spark3', 'spark4']) own(s, QWEN30, { title: 'Qwen3 30B-A3B Instruct 2507', engine: 'vllm', image: IMAGE, args: OWN_ARGS, memoryGiB: 75 });
      // Spark 3: gpt-oss-20b runs as its start from the recorded setup left it (the setup's flags and a memory share).
      swap('spark2', 'sc-gpt-oss-20b|running|Up 1 second|', 'sc-gpt-oss-20b|exited|Exited (0) just now|');
      swap('spark3', 'sc-testorg--Qwen3.8-27B-abliterated-GGUF|running|Up 1 second|', 'sc-testorg--Qwen3.8-27B-abliterated-GGUF|exited|Exited (0) just now|');
      swap('spark3', 'sc-gpt-oss-20b|exited|Exited (0) just now|', 'sc-gpt-oss-20b|running|Up 5 minutes|');
      writeFileSync(m('spark3', 'cmd-sc-gpt-oss-20b'), ranAs('gpt-oss-20b', [...SETUP_ARGS, '--gpu-memory-utilization', '0.5']));
      writeFileSync(join(stateDir, 'verified.json'), JSON.stringify({ deployments: [recorded] }), { mode: 0o600 });
      // Spark 4 (looked at last): Qwen3-30B runs exactly as its own settings say, and has no record yet.
      swap('spark4', `${RADIX}|running|Up 1 second|`, `${RADIX}|exited|Exited (0) just now|`);
      swap('spark4', `sc-${QWEN30}|exited|Exited (0) just now|`, `sc-${QWEN30}|running|Up 5 minutes|`);
      writeFileSync(m('spark4', `cmd-sc-${QWEN30}`), ranAs(QWEN30, [...OWN_ARGS, '--gpu-memory-utilization', '0.85']));
    } });
  }, { timeout: 60000 * SLOW });
  after(() => fleet?.stop());

  test('one started from a Recipes setup keeps only the record its start made; one running as its own settings say is saved once', { timeout: 150000 * SLOW }, async () => {
    const saved = await waitFor(() => runsOf(QWEN30)[0], { timeout: 90000, what: `${QWEN30} saved as a proven run` });
    assert.deepEqual({ engine: saved.engine, image: saved.image, args: saved.args, env: saved.env, sparks: saved.sparks, nodes: saved.nodes, runs: saved.runs },
      { engine: 'vllm', image: IMAGE, args: OWN_ARGS, env: {}, sparks: 1, nodes: ['spark4'], runs: 1 });
    // Spark 3 was looked at before Spark 4: nothing was saved for gpt-oss-20b under its own settings (no flags).
    assert.deepEqual(runsOf('gpt-oss-20b').map(d => d.key), [recorded.key]);
  });
});
```

HOW THE STAND-IN FLEET IS SET UP
- A second fleet, started as if the console had just restarted. Everything is seeded in `prepare`; no start is run.
- Spark 3: sc-gpt-oss-20b is running. Its `.mock/cmd-…` file says it runs with the recorded setup's flags. verified.json holds that setup's run.
- Spark 4: sc-Qwen3-30B is running with exactly its own spark.json flags and has no record.
- Both spark.json files name the stand-in's image, so only the flags differ for gpt-oss-20b.

WHAT IT ASSERTS
- The Spark 4 model is saved once under its own settings. This proves adoption ran and still works.
- gpt-oss-20b has only the setup's entry.

WHY IT FAILS WITHOUT THE CHANGE
- Today's code ignores the new switch and adopts at 60 s. The 90 s wait still reaches the last assert.
- There it finds a second gpt-oss-20b entry with key ["gpt-oss-20b","vllm",IMAGE,1,[],{}], so the assert fails.
- If the comparison is broken so nothing matches, the Spark 4 model is never saved and the wait times out.

specs.spec:
Rule to build: a model across two Sparks is healed for a stopped part only when a part crashed. When every part that is down was stopped (exit 0, or 143, or 137 with Docker's OOMKilled false), it is left stopped and held. A memory kill keeps today's words.

Before editing: re-read `healReason` and `watchSpark` by name (the files are being changed by the apps work). Do not touch `healApp`, 21-apps.mjs, `maybeHeal`, `bringBack`, `desiredNow`, `consoleStopped`, any action, event type or the page.

1. service/src/19-watcher.mjs. Add just above `async function watchSpark(node)`:
```js
// How a container that stopped ended, from the watcher's record: a crash is a memory kill, or an exit code other than a
// stop's own (0, or 143 and 137, which docker stop leaves). One rule for the stopped/crashed events and for healing.
const crashedExit = w => w.oomKilled || (w.exitCode !== null && ![0, 137, 143].includes(w.exitCode));
```
In `watchSpark` replace the line `const crashed = next.oomKilled || (next.exitCode !== null && ![0, 137, 143].includes(next.exitCode));` with `const crashed = crashedExit(next);`. Same logic, no behaviour change.

2. service/src/20-heal.mjs `healReason`, pair branch. After the line `if (gone.some(w => Number(w.exitCode) === 255)) return null;` and before `const ranFor = ...`, insert exactly:
```js
  // The watcher hasn't looked since a part stopped (its record still says running), so how that part ended isn't known
  // yet: wait for its next look, a minute at most, rather than judge from an old record.
  if (gone.some(w => w.running)) return null;
  // Only a crash is healed. When every part that is down was stopped (exit 0, or docker stop's 143 or 137 with no memory
  // kill), someone stopped it: by hand, or a stop of Spark Console's that a restart cut off. It stays stopped and is
  // held, as a one-Spark model is (F24, 2026-11-11). A crashed part among them still brings the pair back.
  if (gone.length && !gone.some(crashedExit)) return { skip: 'it was stopped, not crashed' };
```
Everything else in `healReason` stays as it is and in the same order: the `consoleStopped` line, the out-of-memory skip and its words, the power-on path, the 255 line, the 10-minute rule, the final `why`. `gone.length` keeps today's behaviour when a part's container is missing (not part of F24).

3. Same file, header comment. Line 4: "a model across two Sparks that lost a part" becomes "a model across two Sparks that lost a part to a crash". In the "Never:" sentence add after "one that ran out of memory,": "one whose parts were stopped, not crashed (exit 0, 137 or 143: someone's stop, so it is held, as a one-Spark model is),".

4. Run `node tools/build-server.mjs` and `node tools/code-map.mjs`.

5. Tests and the one stand-in lever (see test).

6. docs/architecture.md and test/README.md (see docs).

7. Gate: `SPARK_TEST_STRICT=1 node --test test/heal-more.test.mjs test/heal-newer.test.mjs test/heal.test.mjs test/build.test.mjs`, then `node test/lock.mjs`. Check that the five workflow versions in service/workflows.lock.json are the same as before (only `build` and `testedAt` change), so no live validation run is needed. The install waits for the owner's yes.

Words the owner sees, in order (all through existing code, no new text elsewhere):
- Activity / fleet_events / Sparkie's recent events: "didn’t restart sc-<model> on spark3: it was stopped, not crashed".
- should_be_running, GET /desired and Sparkie's "Should be running but held" line: held, why "it was stopped, not crashed".
- The one held notice, when it is first seen after that: "sc-<model> on Spark 3 should be running but isn’t (it was stopped, not crashed). Bring it back, or forget it, in Activity."
"Bring it back" (page button, or the owner's word to Sparkie) starts it across both Sparks as today.
